<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jérémie</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jérémie is a port town on the northern coast of the south-western peninsula of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haiti">Haiti</a>, capital of the Grand'Anse <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a>. In the colony of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-domingue">Saint-Domingue</a> it was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a>-growing district, and in September 1793 British troops from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamaica">Jamaica</a> landed there at the invitation of local planters, beginning the British occupation. It was the birthplace of General Thomas-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandre-dumas">Alexandre Dumas</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jeremie.jpg" alt="Jérémie" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bruno Le Bansais · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:J%C3%A9r%C3%A9mie_ha%C3%AFti_3.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Jérémie, in Haitian Creole Jeremi, is a commune and the capital city of the Grand'Anse department in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haiti">Haiti</a>. It is relatively isolated from the rest of the country, and the Grande-Anse River flows near the city. At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> of 2015 it had a population of about 134,317. About 40 nautical miles, or 74 kilometres, to the west lies Navassa Island, which is claimed by both Haiti and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. The city has an airport, Jérémie Airport, whose area is known as Numero Deux, and its districts include Beaudrouin, Campagne, Carrefour Sanon, La Forêt and Rampe des Lions.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>General Thomas-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandre-dumas">Alexandre Dumas</a>, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/criollo-people">Creole</a>, was born here in 1762, the son of a French nobleman and his African slave. His father took him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> at the age of 18, and he became known for fighting under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and as the father of the writer Alexandre Dumas. In 1954 Hurricane Hazel hit Jérémie and destroyed much of the city and many neighbouring inland villages. In 1964, during the event known as the Jérémie Vespers, the Haitian army and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tonton-macoute">Tonton Macoutes</a> massacred 27 people in the city.</p><p>In the early 2000s archaeologists uncovered an ancient synagogue of Crypto-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> in the city, the only one found on the island, and Jérémie has historically been inhabited by many mixed-race families of Jewish descent. After the earthquake of 2010 a food aid convoy that had been delivered to Jérémie Airport met a hijacking attempt by 20 men on 30 January 2010, and medical supplies were also flown in through the airport for injured refugees arriving after the quake. In October 2016 Hurricane Matthew almost completely destroyed the city.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Jérémie is called the city of the poets because of the many writers, poets and historians born there. Among them are the poets Emile Roumer, who lived from 1903 to 1988, and Etzer Vilaire, from 1872 to 1951. Joseph Serge Miot, Archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/port-au-prince">Port-au-Prince</a>, who was killed in the earthquake of 2010, was also born in the city. So was Modeste Testas, an Ethiopian woman who was formerly enslaved and who lived from 1765 to 1870; a statue of her in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bordeaux">Bordeaux</a> commemorates the transatlantic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a>. Elisabeth Dieudonné Vincent, who lived from 1798 to 1883, was a free coloured businesswoman.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Jérémie” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>François Duvalier, distrusting an army he purged repeatedly, built the Tontons Macoutes, a private militia that terrorised the population; the 1964 rising at <b>Jérémie</b> was crushed with public executions.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-haiti">Republic of Haiti · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>British troops from Jamaica landed at <b>Jérémie</b> and Môle Saint-Nicolas in September 1793 and captured Port-au-Prince in June 1794.</blockquote><a href="/state/british-occupation-of-saint-domingue">British occupation of Saint-Domingue</a></li></ul></section>
